Let's assume [entering the LOS of a centaur when you'd rather not be 
fighting these yet] was unavoidable. If you're a spellcaster, you need to 
fight, escape, or close (because centaurs often unwisely choose melee combat 
when they're in melee range). If you need to fight, then you just keep on
blasting and quaffing; each potion of heal wounds spent is a terrible loss,
to be sure, but these are the very situations they were made for. If you
need to escape, it's a question of just teleporting, or scroll-blinking and
then teleporting. Scroll-blinking is a terrible cost, but when the centaur
stands a good chance of killing you in the turn it takes to read the ?oTel,
there's not much else to be done (unless this is because you are wounded
and there's a good chance that a !oHW will remedy this). Unless you're VERY
sure you can cut LOS by rounding a corner, choosing not to scroll-blink
tends to have a deep cost in terms of healing, but again, scrolls of
blinking are rarer and more powerful than !oHW, so it's a tough call. Closing
is a subcase of fighting, just with an interlude before the fight begins.

In the case of a fighting class (inasmuch as "class" exists in Crawl),
fighting means closing, unless you judge it's time to break out an attack
wand... which is sometimes the right decision. Sometimes fighting also means
escaping -- you're willing and able to beat the @#%%@ down in the near future
(rather than, say, after clearing several more levels), but not under such
unfavorable conditions, so you teleport away, maneuver a bit, sneak up
through a back alley, and take your own turn throwing a surprise party for
the @#%@%.

